SC-D: my injection code has a bug: I add `delay[st] += delta` and then apply delay to the F chunk itself plus subsequent. But my reconstruction adds the *cumulative* delay to all subsequent intervals, which is fine, but the injected gap appears as a gap only if the preceding interval ends before the delayed F starts. Since the delay is cumulative and applied to the F chunk itself, there is a gap of delta before it. But then subsequent tasks also shift by the same amount → gap only appears once per injection: good. But the attribution found fwd_hole share 0.085, not >0.5. Recovered time = 0.085*idle. Total idle after injection = base idle 56 + 192 = 248? then 0.085*248 ≈ 21 time units, while injected = 192. So the attribution didn't see most of them as fwd_hole — likely classified as dep_or_comm because at the gap start the forward wasn't ready (upstream F not finished yet) — that's *correct* behavior: if the F isn't ready, delaying it doesn't create a forward hole; it just shifts. Right! Many injections happened on chunks that weren't ready at that moment (stage idle waiting on dependency), so delaying them creates "dep wait", not "forward hole". That's the analyzer being correct and my *check* being wrong (my expectation was naive).

Better injection test: inject idle only at moments when a forward is provably ready (i.e., choose gaps where attribution of the *original* trace shows the stage was in dep_or_comm... no — choose a gap and delay the next F only if it is ready at gap start). Cleanest: force idle at times when the stage has a ready F: e.g., take a gap where the *next* interval is an F that was ready at gap start (classify original trace, pick gaps classified as... in the baseline ZB(D=2p) trace, gaps are dep/comm/fill/drain, so no ready-F gaps exist).

Alternative proper test of instrument sensitivity: run a *deliberately crippled scheduler* — e.g., a policy that runs W before F (starving forwards) or a "1F1B-order but with ZB work split", which should produce measurable fwd_hole. Simplest robust sensitivity control: run ZB with a wrong priority (W first) and confirm attribution reports a large fwd_hole share; plus run 1F1B and confirm the known (p−1)/(m+p−1) bubble shows up as idle with the correct magnitude. That is a genuine sensitivity test with a *known* injected defect.

Even better: implement "ZB with random idle injection at ready moments" — pick gaps in a trace where an F *is* ready. To create such gaps, use a scheduler variant that idles deliberately: add config param `idle_probe_every` — no, keep it simpler.

Design: sensitivity test = "corrupt the schedule by swapping two adjacent tasks on a stage so that a ready F is pushed behind a W" — automatic, guaranteed to create forward holes. Simplest deterministic corruption: sort each stage's task list to put all W tasks earlier (i.e., re-run simulator with priority W-first). That yields known structure: forwards delayed behind weight grads → fwd_hole large. Let me implement `priority_mode` in SimConfig: 'zb' (A>F>W), 'wfirst' (W>A>F?) hmm W-first will block because W ready only after A... In a W-first policy, whenever a W is ready it runs before F → forwards stall → gaps classified fwd_hole whenever an F is ready at the gap start. That should produce substantial fwd_hole share. 

Let me implement: SimConfig.priority_mode ∈ {"zb","wfirst","a_first_only"}; and test that attribution recovers fwd_hole share > 0.3 for wfirst while zb gives ~0. This demonstrates sensitivity (the analyzer distinguishes a policy that creates forward holes from one that doesn't) — and is a legitimate control-design analogue: "C1-style sensitivity: known-defect schedule must be detected".

