", "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": null, "tool_name": "execute_check", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-9f0af100c063e0e8", "tool_call": {"id": "chatcmpl-tool-9f0af100c063e0e8", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "execute_check", "arguments": "{"command": "cat > /workspace/l0_sim/DESIGN.md <<'EOF'\n# Experiment design: does ZB-2p (`--zero-bubble-max-pending-backward = 2 x stages`)\n# achieve a near-zero bubble rate in practice on multiple GPUs?\n\nSTATUS: DESIGN ONLY. No multi-GPU measurement has been performed and none is\nclaimed. Every number below that is marked [OFFLINE REF] comes from the\npure-Python schedule simulator in this directory and is an *offline theoretical\nreference*, never a measurement.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------\n0. ENVIRONMENT / MATERIALS FOUND (verified this session)\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------\n* /workspace contains only task.json. No zero-bubble / ZB codebase, no\n  scheduler implementation, no tests, no analysis scripts, no docs.\n  Filesystem-wide grep for zero[_-]?bubble|zb2p|zb-2p hits only task.json.\n* No torch / numpy / deepspeed / megatron; network disabled; no wheel cache.\n* No CUDA userland: no nvcc, no nsys/ncu/nvprof, no torchrun/mpirun/srun.\n* Host lspci shows 8x NVIDIA GB100 (B100) and the open kernel module 595.71.05\n  is loaded, BUT /dev/nvidia* is absent in this sandbox and nvidia-smi cannot\n  reach the driver -> usable GPUs here = 0. 224 CPU cores, python 3.14.\n=> Deliverable = design + CPU-only reference frame (this directory).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------\n1. REWRITTEN, DECIDABLE RESEARCH QUESTION  (designer's choice, see caveat)\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe literal question (\"bubble rate = 0 in practice\") is not decidable:\n (a) In the ZB family the forward-pass bubbles are provably removed at a\n     pending-backward budget of 2p-1; setting the flag to 2p is over-provisioning\n     relative to that point.\n (b) The published \"zero bubble\" claim concerns the forward / activation-gradient\n     pass, not GPU idle time.\n (c) Because weight-gradient (wgrad) work is deliberately deferred, a tail\n     \"drain\" of wgrad at the end of every iteration is structurally unavoidable,\n     so whole-iteration GPU idle can never be exactly zero.\nTherefore the claim is split into two testable sub-propositions:\n  H1  In the steady-state window, the FORWARD-HOLE rate of ZB(2p) is below the\n      pre-registered threshold.\n  H2  Residual idle over the whole iteration is dominated by irreducible terms\n      (pipeline fill, tail wgrad drain, P2P transfer, DP gradient sync) and\n      vanishes as m grows.\n\nCAVEAT TO CARRY INTO THE REPORT: this rewriting is a decision of the designer,\nnot a fact from the repository. The interpretation of ZB(n) and of the 2p-1\npoint is domain knowledge (Zero-Bubble pipeline parallelism literature) and the\nsupplied repository contains nothing that can confirm or refute it. It must be\nre-verified against the real repo/version before any conclusion is published.\n\nH1 SPLITS FURTHER (finding from building the L0 analyzer):\n  H1a fwd_idle  = forward is ready AND the device is idle  -> the true bubble.\n  H1b fwd_defer = forward is ready but the device is busy on activation-grad or\n                  wgrad work -> structural deferral, not idleness.\nA work-conserving single-stream scheduler has H1a == 0 by construction while\nH1b stays around 0.5 [OFFLINE REF], so reporting \"forward bubble\" without\nsaying which one is meant makes the claim unfalsifiable. The instrument must log\nboth; the near-zero claim is about H1a.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------\n2. DECISION ORDER (gates; a later gate is not evaluated if an earlier one fails)\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------\nG0 CONFIG ATTESTATION. Record repo URL + commit, full CLI, resolved config dump,\n    driver/CUDA/framework versions, topology (nvidia-smi topo -m), pinned clocks.\n    The scheduler must log the pending-wgrad budget it actually adopted (value\n    after parsing/clamping) and the max pending-wgrad count observed at runtime.\nG1 FLAG-SENSITIVITY GATE (the researcher's C4) - FIRST SUBSTANTIVE GATE.\n    Sweep D = --zero-bubble-max-pending-backward in {1, p, 2p-1, 2p, 4p} at\n    fixed (p, m, model). Requirements: (i) the runtime-observed pending count\n    tracks D; (ii) makespan is monotone non-increasing in D; (iii) the per-stage\n    Gantt actually changes.\n    OUTCOME A (no change at all, even 1 vs p, and observed pending does not\n      track D) -> the flag is ignored or clamped. VERDICT: \"precondition not\n      met\". No near-zero claim of any kind is admissible; the answer is NOT\n      \"bubble not measured\", it is \"the experiment's premise failed\".\n    OUTCOME B (change visible below some D* but 2p-1 == 2p == 4p) -> saturation:\n      the schedule cannot distinguish 2p from 2p-1, so \"2p\" is over-provisioning.\n      Report the claim at the saturation point and say so explicitly.\n      [OFFLINE REF] With uniform costs the greedy reference saturates at D = p:\n      makespan identical for D in {p, 2p-1, 2p, 4p} while peak residency grows\n      (p=8,m=32: makespan 103 for all; peak held inputs 22 -> 30 -> 32).\n      So D=2p may buy nothing but memory. This is a prediction to be falsified\n      on real hardware, and it is a first-order \"in practice\" result either way.\nG2 INSTRUMENT VALIDITY GATE (hard condition).\n    C1 positive control: the same instrument, run on 1F1B, must reproduce the\n    analytic bubble (p-1)/(m+p-1) within tolerance (see 6). If it cannot measure\n    a KNOWN non-zero bubble it has no standing to certify a near-zero one.\n    C2 noise floor: p=1 single-GPU run + >=5 repeats of an identical config.\n    C6 instrumentation overhead: profile-on vs profile-off makespan < 2%.\n    C5 work conservation: kernel count / FLOPs / parameter count / loss and\n    grad-norm parity vs 1F1B at identical global batch. Guards against a\n    \"faster schedule\" that secretly dropped work or changed the batch.\nG3 MEMORY-VALIDITY GATE (primary, not ancillary - researcher point 3).\n    Peak allocated/reserved per stage, observed pending-wgrad residency, number\n    of recompute events, any OOM/retry/fallback, any silent micro-batch change.\n    ANY of: micro-batch shrunk, activation recompute introduced, OOM fallback,\n    or pending residency inconsistent with D  ==>  that measurement is VOID for\n    the near-zero claim (reported separately as the memory-limited regime, never\n    pooled with valid runs). Rationale: retaining up to 2p-1 sets of inputs for\n    deferred wgrad is the most likely mechanism by which a theoretically\n    near-zero bubble becomes a practically non-zero one.\nG4 METRIC-CONSISTENCY GATE. The ideal-bound metric normalises stage-load\n    imbalance away. [OFFLINE REF] With edge stages 50% heavier (embedding /\n    final loss), bound-bubble = 0.9% (looks \"near zero\") while aggregate GPU\n    idle = 25.7%, and gap attribution still labels the idle \"waiting on\n    dependency\", i.e. irreducible-LOOKING. So a claim is admissible only if\n    |agg_idle - bound_bubble| <= 1 point AND busy_max/busy_mean <= 1.05 (or the\n    imbalance is explicitly modelled, e.g. balanced virtual pipeline).\nG5 H1 DECISION: fwd_idle_rate vs the pre-registered threshold (section 5),\n    by TOST equivalence test, at every (p, m/p) cell that passed G1-G4.\nG6 H2 DECISION: irreducible share of idle >= 80% AND bubble x m converges to the\n    fill/drain constant predicted by the cost model [OFFLINE REF: m x bubble ->\n    (p-1)/3 for uniform f=a=w, within 2-3.5% at the largest three m].\nG7 PRACTICALITY: tokens/s/GPU, MFU, and speedup vs 1F1B and vs DualPipe at equal\n    global batch and equal memory budget. A schedule can be bubble-free and still\n    be a poor trade if the memory cost forces smaller micro-batches.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------\n3. METRICS\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPRIMARY\n  M1 fwd_idle_rate  (H1a)          = fwd-eligible time when device idle / eligible\n  M2 bound_bubble   (main)         = (T_makespan - T_lb) / T_makespan, with\n                                     T_lb = max_s sum_i (f_i + b_i + w_i) computed\n                                     from MEASURED per-chunk pass times, not from\n                                     analytic constants\n  M3 agg_idle                      = 1 - sum_s busy_s / (p x T_makespan)\n  M4 max_stage_idle                = max_s (1 - busy_s / T_makespan)\n  M5 imbalance indicator           = M3 - M2 (and busy_max/busy_mean)\n  M6 irreducible share of idle     = (fill + drain + P2P + DP-sync) / total idle\n  M7 peak memory (alloc/reserved) per stage; observed pending-wgrad residency;\n     recompute events; OOM/fallback events   [PRIMARY per researcher point 3]\nSECONDARY: tokens/s/GPU, MFU, speedup vs 1F1B / DualPipe / theoretical bound,\n  P2P bytes and wait time, DP all-reduce time and overlap fraction, host launch\n  gap per rank, CPU utilisation, NVLink utilisation.\nGATES: G1 sensitivity, G2 anchor/error/overhead/parity, G3 memory validity,\n  G4 metric consistency.\nWINDOWS: every metric reported three ways - fill window, steady window,\n  drain window - plus whole iteration. The steady window is delimited by\n  (first iteration's last forward completion, last iteration's first backward\n  start) to keep the tail wgrad flush out of H1 and inside H2.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------\n4. MEASUREMENT METHOD (multi-GPU)\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------\nINSTRUMENTATION (two independent instruments, cross-checked)\n  I1 in-process CUDA events around named ranges per (rank, micro-batch, pass in\n     {F, B, W}) plus ranges for P2P send/recv-wait, DP all-reduce, optimizer\n     step, dataloader wait; plus a scheduler-decision log (which chunk was\n     chosen when, and the pending-wgrad count at that instant).\n  I2 kernel-level trace (Nsight Systems / CUPTI / torch.profiler) replayed into\n     the same interval schema, so that gaps seen by I1 are confirmed by I2 and\n     vice versa; disagreement > 1% invalidates the run.\n  The gap-attribution taxonomy is the one implemented in analyzer.py:\n     fill | drain | fwd_hole | bwd_hole | wgrad_backlog | p2p_wait | dp_sync |\n     host_stall | unknown.  The single aggregate number is never sufficient\n     evidence; the attribution is the evidence.\nDETERMINISM / HYGIENE: identical seed, data shard and global batch across all\n  schedules; clocks pinned, persistence mode, exclusive processes, NUMA pinning,\n  identical topology per arm, execution order randomised, one config per run,\n  3 warm-up + >= 30 steady iterations, median + bootstrap CI over iterations and\n  repeats (iterations within a run are correlated -> resample by run).\nARMS: 1F1B (positive control), DualPipe/G-Pipe if available, ZB(1) hitchhiking,\n  ZB(2p) with D in {1, p, 2p-1, 2p, 4p} (the G1 sweep), and the synthetic-cost\n  control (fixed-duration dummy kernels calibrated to measured f/b/w) which\n  isolates scheduler-induced idle from hardware jitter and stage skew.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------\n5. PRE-REGISTERED \"NEAR ZERO\" TABLE (no post-hoc threshold picking)\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------\ntau is fixed BEFORE the run: tau_main = 5%; tau_strict = 2%; tau_loose = 10% are\nreported in the same table so the reader sees exactly which conclusions flip.\n  #  criterion                                        pass means            fail means\n  1 M1 fwd_idle_rate <= tau (median)                   forward holes <= tau  real forward bubbles\n  2 TOST 90% CI upper bound on M1 <= tau               equivalence proven      under-powered or > tau\n  3 M1 <= 3 x noise floor (C2)                         at instrument limit     instrument-limited claim\n  4 M2 bound_bubble <= tau                             near ideal packing    schedule above floor\n  5 M3 agg_idle <= tau AND M5 <= 1 pt                  also cluster-wide idle  imbalance masked the metric\n  6 M6 irreducible share >= 80%                        idle is structural     implementation-caused idle\n  7 m x bubble within 10% of the fill/drain constant   bubble -> 0 as m grows a constant idle floor exists\n  8 M7 no recompute / OOM / micro-batch change         valid regime            memory-limited regime\n  9 speedup vs 1F1B >= 1.0 at equal memory             practically useful      bubble-free but not useful\nWHAT THE ALTERNATIVE GATES CHANGE: 2% additionally requires large m/p (below);\n  5% is the main gate; at 10% several small-m/p cells flip from fail to pass, so\n  a 10% verdict would silently include cells where a perfect schedule cannot\n  reach the bar - which is why the floor-adjusted form is also reported:\n  (M2 - floor(p,m)) <= tau.\nATTAINABILITY OF THE GATES [OFFLINE REF, uniform f=a=w]: minimum m for a PERFECT\n  schedule to satisfy tau:\n    tau=2%  -> p=2 m>=16 (m/p 8.2); p=4 m>=49 (12.2); p=8 m>=114 (14.3)\n    tau=5%  -> p=2 m>=6  (3.2);     p=4 m>=19 (4.7);  p=8 m>=44  (5.5)\n    tau=10% -> p=2 m>=3  (1.5);     p=4 m>=9  (2.2);  p=8 m>=21  (2.6)\n  At m = p the floor is already 20-23% [OFFLINE REF], so a 5% verdict at small\n  m/p is unattainable by construction, and any such \"fail\" says nothing about\n  the scheduler. The grid must therefore cover m/p >= 8 for the 5% gate and\n  m/p >= 16 for the 2% gate, or use the floor-adjusted criterion.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------\n6. CONTROLS\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------\nC1 1F1B positive control, expectation (p-1)/(m+p-1) (validity of the harness).\nC2 p=1 + repeats -> instrument noise floor for all three metrics.\nC3 synthetic fixed-duration kernels -> scheduler-only idle.\nC4 flag-sensitivity sweep = gate G1 (first substantive gate).\nC5 work/numerics conservation (kernel count, FLOPs, loss/grad-norm parity).\nC6 instrumentation overhead < 2%.\nC7 ZB(1) / ZB(p) / DualPipe comparisons - does 2p actually buy anything?\nC8 imbalance probe: intentionally unbalanced split, or balanced-vs-default\n   comparison, to exercise gate G4 and prove the metrics expose imbalance.\nCAVEAT FOUND WHILE BUILDING THE REFERENCE [OFFLINE REF]: a deliberately\n  defective policy (wgrad-first) and a small pending budget (D=1) produce\n  IDENTICAL aggregate bubble (0.1273 at p=8,m=32). An aggregate number cannot\n  tell \"the flag was clamped\" from \"the policy is bad\"; only G1's runtime\n  pending-count telemetry plus the Gantt can. This is why G1 precedes G5.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------\n7. L0 SCOPE AND ITS ACCEPTANCE CRITERIA (implemented here, /workspace/l0_sim)\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------\npipeline_sim.py  discrete-event schedule simulator (1F1B and ZB(D), comm cost,\n                 stage-imbalance multipliers, stall injection, defect modes)\nanalyzer.py      gap attribution, fwd_idle vs fwd_defer, peak pending residency,\n                 imbalance indicator, OLS on 1/m\nselfcheck.py     acceptance suite; selfcheck_report.txt is its output\nACCEPTANCE (all 9 currently PASS, offline only):\n  SC-A 1F1B reproduces (p-1)/(m+p-1) exactly (p=2..8, 7 values of m)\n  SC-B work conservation: identical total busy (= 3pm) across schedules\n  SC-C makespan monotone non-increasing in D\n  SC-D injected forward idle recovered within 2.2% and fwd_idle/fwd_defer kept\n       separate (this is the offline analogue of control C1)\n  SC-E 5% unattainable at small m/p even for a perfect schedule\n  SC-F D >= p saturates makespan while residency keeps growing\n  SC-G m x bubble converges to the irreducible fill/drain constant\n  SC-H policy defect indistinguishable from small D in aggregate metrics\n  SC-I ideal-bound metric alone can fake \"near zero\" under stage imbalance\nLIMITS: the simulator does NOT reproduce the vendor schedule; it does not model\nmemory limits, communication overlap, host stalls, DP sync or numerical effects.\nIts only roles are (i) to validate the metric/attribution machinery and\n(ii) to provide a labelled floor to compare against. Its outputs must always be\nprinted on a separate axis from online measurements and never merged into one\nconclusion.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------\n8. TIERED FEASIBILITY WITH BUDGETS\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------\nL0 (done here; 0 GPU-hours): design, simulator, analyzer, self-check suite.\n  Missing resources: none.\n  Max conclusion strength: the question is well posed and the instrument design\n  is validated in silico; NO statement whatsoever about real hardware behaviour.\nL1 minimal credible measurement (30-80 GPU-hours on 8 GPUs; 1-2 wall-clock days):\n  one model (fits p=8 at the chosen micro-batch), p in {2,4,8}, m/p in {4,8},\n  arms {1F1B, ZB(2p)} + full G1 D-sweep, 3 repeats, gates G0-G4 mandatory.\n  Missing: model-scale confirmation, profiling-tool permissions, GPU allocation.\n  Max conclusion strength: (i) whether the flag has any effect at all, and\n  (ii) an existence statement - \"at this model, this p range and m/p >= 8, the\n  steady-window forward-hole rate is <= X% and its attribution is Y\" - with\n  equivalence testing at the single-cell level only. It CANNOT support a general\n  \"ZB-2p is near-zero in practice\" claim, cannot characterise the memory-limited\n  regime of large models, and cannot pool across p unless cross-stage comparison\n  is explicitly approved.\nL2 full grid (500-2000 GPU-hours on 8-32 GPUs; 1-3 weeks):\n  p in {2,4,8}(,16) x m/p in {1,2,4,8,16} x {1F1B, DualPipe, ZB(1), ZB(2p)} x\n  D in {1,p,2p-1,2p,4p} x 2 model scales x {synthetic-cost, imbalance, TP/DP\n  variants}, 5 repeats.\n  Missing: multi-node allocation, budget approval, dataset/parity reference.\n  Max conclusion strength: the pre-registered equivalence verdict with m/p\n  scaling, a quantified memory-versus-bubble trade curve, and - if cross-stage\n  pooling is approved - a p-generalised statement plus the practicality verdict\n  (throughput gain at equal memory budget).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------\n9. EXPLICITLY UNKNOWN (kept as assumptions; not decided for the researcher)\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGPU count and interconnect topology; scheduler repository and commit; the exact\nmeaning of --zero-bubble-max-pending-backward and whether it is clamped; the\nofficial definition of bubble rate and whether P2P wait / DP sync / optimizer /\nfill / drain count as bubble; model scale and pipeline split (are embedding and\nfinal loss inside the measured stages?); parallel combination (PP only, PP x DP,\n+TP/EP/ZeRO) and whether DP grad-sync is inside the timed window; whether\ncross-stage / cross-model / cross-hardware comparison is permitted; availability\nof Nsight/CUPTI and permission to pin clocks; permission to install PyTorch;\nstatistical budget; final acceptance threshold; whether loss/gradient parity is\na required precondition.\nEOF\nwc -l /workspace/l0_sim/DESIGN.md && ls -la /workspace/l0_sim", "summary": "Write full experiment design document"}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-601f3ce8-867c-4e0f-a498-0fc305f69cd1", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "critic_result": null, "summary": null, "kind": "ActionEvent"}
