  M4 max_stage_idle       = max_s (1 - busy_s / T_makespan)
  M5 imbalance indicator  = M3 - M2 (plus busy_max/busy_mean)
  M6 irreducible share    = (fill + drain + P2P + DP-sync) / total idle
  M7 peak memory (alloc/reserved) per stage; observed pending-wgrad residency;
     recompute events; OOM/fallback events                 [PRIMARY per point 3]
SECONDARY: tokens/s/GPU, MFU, speedup vs 1F1B / DualPipe / theoretical bound,
  P2P bytes and wait time, DP all-reduce time and overlap fraction, host launch
  gap per rank, CPU utilisation, NVLink utilisation.
GATES: G1 sensitivity, G2 anchor/error/overhead/parity, G3 memory validity,
  G4 metric consistency.
WINDOWS: every metric reported over fill window, steady window, drain window and
  whole iteration. The steady window excludes the tail wgrad flush, which is kept
  inside H2 instead of H1.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
4. MEASUREMENT METHOD (multi-GPU)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
INSTRUMENTATION (two independent instruments, cross-checked)
  I1 in-process CUDA events around named ranges per (rank, micro-batch, pass in
     {F, B, W}) plus P2P send / recv-wait, DP all-reduce, optimizer step,
     dataloader wait; plus a scheduler-decision log (chunk chosen, pending-wgrad
     count at that instant).
  I2 kernel-level trace (Nsight Systems / CUPTI / torch.profiler) replayed into
     the same interval schema; gaps seen by I1 must be confirmed by I2; a
     disagreement > 1% invalidates the run.
  Gap-attribution taxonomy (implemented in analyzer.py): fill | drain | fwd_hole |
  bwd_hole | wgrad_backlog | p2p_wait | dp_sync | host_stall | unknown. The single
  aggregate number is never sufficient evidence; the attribution is the evidence.
DETERMINISM / HYGIENE: identical seed, data shard and global batch across arms;
  clocks pinned, persistence mode, exclusive processes, NUMA pinning, identical
  topology per arm, randomised execution order, one config per run, 3 warm-up +
  >= 30 steady iterations, median + bootstrap CI resampled BY RUN (iterations
  within a run are correlated).
ARMS: 1F1B (positive control); DualPipe/G-Pipe if available; ZB(1) hitchhiking;
  ZB(2p) with D in {1, p, 2p-1, 2p, 4p} (the G1 sweep); and a synthetic-cost arm
  (fixed-duration dummy kernels calibrated to measured f/b/w) that isolates
  scheduler-induced idle from hardware jitter and stage skew.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
5. PRE-REGISTERED "NEAR ZERO" TABLE (no post-hoc threshold picking)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
tau fixed BEFORE the run: tau_main = 5%; tau_strict = 2% and tau_loose = 10%
reported in the same table so the reader sees exactly which conclusions flip.
 #  criterion                                     pass means            fail means
 1  M1 fwd_idle_rate <= tau (median)               fwd holes <= tau       real forward bubbles
 2  TOST 90% CI upper bound on M1 <= tau           equivalence proven     under-powered or > tau
 3  M1 <= 3 x noise floor (C2)                     at instrument limit    instrument-limited claim
 4  M2 bound_bubble <= tau                         near ideal packing     schedule above floor
 5  M3 agg_idle <= tau AND M5 <= 1 pt              also cluster-wide idle imbalance masked metric
 6  M6 irreducible share >= 80%                    idle is structural     implementation-caused idle
 7  m x bubble within 10% of fill/drain constant   bubble -> 0 as m grows a constant idle floor
 8  M7 no recompute / OOM / micro-batch change     valid regime           memory-limited regime
 9  speedup vs 1F1B >= 1.0 at equal memory         practically useful     bubble-free but useless
Row 9 fails often even when 1-8 pass; that is a legitimate "near-zero but not
useful" outcome and must be reported as such rather than hidden.
ATTAINABILITY OF THE GATES [OFFLINE REF, uniform f=a=w]: minimum m for a PERFECT
schedule to satisfy tau:
  tau=2%  -> p=2 m>=16 (m/p 8.2); p=4 m>=49 (12.2); p=8 m>=114 (14.3)
  tau=5%  -> p=2 m>=6  (3.2);     p=4 m>=19 (4.7);  p=8 m>=44  (5.5)
  tau=10% -> p=2 m>=3  (1.5);     p=4 m>=9  (2.2);  p=8 m>=21  (2.6)
At m = p the floor is already 20-23% [OFFLINE REF], so a 5% verdict at small m/p
is unattainable by construction and any such "fail" says nothing about the
scheduler. Hence the grid must cover m/p >= 8 for the 5% gate and m/p >= 16 for
the 2% gate, or use the floor-adjusted criterion (M2 - floor(p,m)) <= tau.
Choosing 10% instead of 5% flips several small-m/p cells from fail to pass, which
is precisely the post-hoc selection this table is meant to prevent.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
6. CONTROLS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
C1 1F1B positive control, expectation (p-1)/(m+p-1) (harness validity).
C2 p=1 + repeats -> instrument noise floor for all three metrics.
C3 synthetic fixed-duration kernels -> scheduler-only idle.
C4 flag-sensitivity sweep = gate G1 (first substantive gate).
C5 work/numerics conservation (kernel count, FLOPs, loss/grad-norm parity).
C6 instrumentation overhead < 2%.
C7 ZB(1) / ZB(p) / DualPipe comparisons - does 2p actually buy anything?
C8 imbalance probe (intentionally unbalanced split, or balanced vs default split)
   to exercise gate G4 and prove the metrics expose imbalance.
CAVEAT FOUND WHILE BUILDING THE REFERENCE [OFFLINE REF]: a deliberately defective
policy (wgrad-first) and a small pending budget (D=1) give IDENTICAL aggregate
bubble (0.1273 at p=8, m=32). An aggregate number cannot distinguish "flag
clamped" from "policy is bad"; only G1's runtime pending-count telemetry plus the
Gantt can. That is why G1 precedes G5.
EOF
